window.AG_CHAPTERS["16"] = {"id":"16","title":"The Colour on His Sleeve","titleZh":"袖上的顏色","patient":{"name":"Troupe Master Yan 嚴班主","sprite":"man"},"scene":{"img":"art/scene-treatment.jpg","key":"rain"},"pattern":"Liver Fire Insulting the Lung 肝火犯肺","principle":"cool the Liver, quiet the Lung, and stop the bleeding","tongue":"red, especially at the edges, with a yellow coating","pulseNote":{"en":"Taut as a plucked string, and hurrying — you can feel the whole length of it at once.","zh":"脈來繃急如張弦，且數，舉之一線俱應"},"needleAsk":{"en":"Needle <b>K&#466;ngzu&#236; LU6</b>. Tap its exact location.","zh":"行針 — 點出穴位精確位置"},"target":{"id":"LU6","zh":"孔最","py":"K&#466;ngzu&#236;","x":152,"y":364,"img":"art/body-forearm-palmar.jpg","w":434,"h":1159,"view":{"x":84,"y":248,"w":224,"h":448},"decoys":[{"x":152,"y":300},{"x":152,"y":452},{"x":120,"y":364},{"x":190,"y":368},{"x":140,"y":652}],"note":"<b>孔最 K&#466;ngzu&#236; LU6</b> — seven cun above the wrist crease, on the line joining Ch&#464;z&#233; LU5 and T&#224;iyu&#257;n LU9. Cleft point of the Lung. Cleft points treat acute and painful conditions, and on the yin channels they treat bleeding — which makes this the point for coughing blood.<br>腕橫紋上七寸，尺澤與太淵連線上。肺之郄穴。郄穴主急症痛症，陰經郄穴兼主血證，故咳血取之。"},"hints":{"ask":{"en":"He told you what he coughed up. Ask what he was doing in the hour before it.","zh":"其人已言所咳者何。當問未咳之前一時，其人何為。"},"look":{"en":"Look at the <b>edges</b> of the tongue, not the middle. The edges have an owner.","zh":"當觀舌之兩<b>邊</b>，不在其中。邊自有所屬。"},"pulse":{"en":"Two qualities, and each names a different organ&#39;s trouble. Take them one at a time.","zh":"脈見二象，各主一臟之病。分而候之。"},"dx":{"en":"Decide full or empty first. Then follow the blood back to its cause. Only then choose which organ you are treating.","zh":"先決虛實，次溯血之所由，末乃定所治何臟。"},"rx":{"en":"One point to hold the bleeding, one to bring the fire down. They are not on the same channel.","zh":"一穴以止其血，一穴以降其火。不在一經。"},"tech":{"en":"There is fire and there is blood. Two reasons to be certain about warmth.","zh":"有火而見血。二者皆令溫法可疑。"}},"reactions":[{"who":"master","en":"Master Shen looks at the sleeve before he looks at the man.","zh":"參師父先看袖子，後看人。"},{"who":"patient","en":"Troupe Master Yan folds the sleeve inward, as if the stain were bad manners.","zh":"嚴班主把袖口向內折起，彷彿那痕跡是失禮。"},{"who":"boy","en":"The herb boy stops sweeping. He has seen the troupe perform.","zh":"藥童停下掃帚。他看過這個班子的戲。"},{"who":"master","en":"\"How many of your players did you shout at this week?\"","zh":"「這一旬，你罵了幾個角兒？」"},{"who":"patient","en":"\"All of them.\" A pause. \"The drummer twice.\"","zh":"「都罵過。」他頓了頓。「鼓佬罵了兩回。」"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Then you have already told the apprentice half the answer. Let him find the other half.\"","zh":"「那你已把一半答案告訴徒弟了。餘下一半，讓他自己尋。」"}],"remedy":{"ask":"excess_def","look":"cold_heat","pulse":"cold_heat","dx":"excess_def","rx":"channel_organ","tech":"excess_def"},"reward":{"cardEn":"Wood Insults Metal","cardZh":"木侮金 · 清肝寧肺","cardTxt":"Wood is normally kept in check by Metal. When Liver fire rises hard enough it turns and scorches the organ that should have restrained it — the reverse direction, called insulting rather than overacting. The English examination word is the trap: Liver invading the Spleen or Stomach is <i>overacting</i>; Liver fire reaching the Lung is <i>insulting</i>. The fire scorches the delicate vessels of the Lung and blood appears in the cough. Cool the Liver and the Lung stops bleeding — treat the Lung alone and it will bleed again after the next argument.","herbId":"gancao","herbEn":"Licorice Root","herbZh":"甘草","herbImg":"art/card-gancao.jpg","sprite":"art/sprite-renshen-jump.webp","herbTxt":"Sweet and neutral, said to reach all twelve channels. Here it does the quiet work: it moistens the Lung and eases the cough, and it harmonises a formula in which cold bitter herbs are cooling two organs at once — without it that combination is hard on the middle burner. Worth knowing that at high doses or over long periods it has real cautions, so it is a herb prescribed with attention rather than added by habit."},"endings":{"three":["Eight days later there is no more blood, and the ribs have stopped burning. He has also, he mentions, stopped shouting at the drummer.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You treated the organ that was angry, not the organ that was bleeding. That is the whole of it. And notice he changed something himself — for this pattern that is half the prescription.\"","八日後，血止，脅下灼痛亦除。他順口提起，也不再罵鼓佬了。<br><b>參師父：</b>「你治的是發怒之臟，非出血之臟。要義盡於此。且看他自改其一事——於本證，此即半劑藥也。」"],"two":["The blood has stopped but the ribs still burn when he is crossed.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You reached the Lung. Go back and ask what was pushing it.\"","血已止，然遇拂逆則脅下仍灼。<br><b>參師父：</b>「你及於肺矣。回頭問：何物推之？」"],"one":["He coughs again on the way out, and looks at his sleeve before he looks back at you.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"Direction. You had the two organs and you had them the wrong way round.\"","將出門而復咳，先看其袖，後回顧你。<br><b>參師父：</b>「方向。二臟你都認得，惟其向倒置。」"],"titles":["Troupe Master Yan Takes the Stage","Troupe Master Yan Rests His Voice","Troupe Master Yan Coughs Again"]},"script":[{"w":"master","en":"Blood in a cough will frighten you into treating the Lung. Wait until you know why it is there.","cn":"見咳血者，多驚而急治其肺。且待知其所以然，而後治之。"},{"w":"patient","en":"It is nothing. A little colour on the sleeve, twice this week. I have a company to run.","cn":"沒什麼。袖子上帶了點顏色，這一旬兩回。我一個班子要顧。"},{"w":"patient","en":"Here — under this rib — it burns. Not a stab. Like something left a lamp lit in there.","cn":"這裡——這根肋骨下面——是灼的。不是刺痛，像有人在裡頭點著盞燈沒吹熄。"},{"w":"patient","en":"And I am angry all day. My head aches from it. Everything is bitter in my mouth by noon.","cn":"我整天在火頭上。頭脹得疼。到晌午，嘴裡什麼都是苦的。"},{"w":"master","en":"Anger, a bitter mouth, a burning rib, and blood in the cough. Two organs. Ask him which one started it.","cn":"怒、口苦、脅灼、咳血。二臟之事。問他：起於何臟？"}],"qa":[{"q":"When the blood comes — what were you doing just before?","cq":"見血之前，適作何事？","good":1,"a":"Both times, straight after a row. Once with the drummer, once with the man who owns the theatre.","ca":"兩回都是剛吵完架。一回是跟鼓佬，一回是跟戲園子的東家。","clue":{"en":"Bleeding triggered by outbursts of anger","zh":"每因大怒而見血"},"note":"This single answer decides the chapter. Anger is the emotion of the Liver, and blood that appears reliably <i>after</i> anger is blood driven by Liver fire, not blood leaking from a weak Spleen or a Lung dried out by yin deficiency. In the English examinations, hemoptysis plus rage is a one-symptom lock on this pattern. 一答而定全章。怒為肝志，血必隨怒而見者，肝火迫血也，非脾不統血，亦非肺陰虛之絡損。英文考題中，咳血兼暴怒，一症即鎖本證。"},{"q":"Describe the phlegm.","cq":"其痰何狀？","good":1,"a":"Yellow, thick, sticks in the throat. Sometimes streaked.","ca":"黃而稠，黏在喉間。有時帶血絲。","clue":{"en":"Thick yellow phlegm, blood-streaked","zh":"痰黃稠，帶血絲"},"note":"Yellow and thick is heat that has been there long enough to congeal fluid. Compare the thin white phlegm of chapter one — same organ, opposite thermal state, and the phlegm alone separates them. 黃而稠者，熱久而煉液成痰。與第一章之痰稀白相較：同一臟，而寒熱相反，但憑痰色質即可分。"},{"q":"Where exactly does the burning sit, and does it move?","cq":"灼痛確在何處？移否？","good":1,"a":"Right side, under the ribs. It does not move. It gets worse when I am angry and better after I sleep.","ca":"右邊肋下。不移。怒則甚，睡後稍減。","clue":{"en":"Fixed burning pain in the right hypochondrium, worse with anger","zh":"右脅下灼痛固定，因怒而甚"},"note":"The hypochondrium is Liver and Gallbladder territory — the channels pass through it. <i>Burning</i> rather than distending tells you the constraint has already turned to fire; plain Liver qi stagnation gives a moving, distending, sighing kind of pain. 脅肋為肝膽所過。痛而<b>灼</b>者，鬱已化火；若但氣滯，則其痛為脹、為走竄，且善太息。"},{"q":"Your eyes are red. Is that new?","cq":"目赤，新起否？","good":1,"a":"Since spring. My wife says I look like the painted general in the second act.","ca":"開春就這樣。內人說我像第二折裡那個花臉將軍。","clue":{"en":"Red eyes, headache, dizziness","zh":"目赤、頭脹痛、眩暈"},"note":"The Liver opens into the eyes, and fire rises — so red eyes, a distending headache and dizziness belong together as fire going upward along the Liver channel. All three are above the diaphragm, which is where fire goes. 肝開竅於目，火性炎上，故目赤、頭脹痛、眩暈三者同屬肝火上炎。三症皆在膈上，火之所趨也。"},{"q":"How are you sleeping, and how is your temper on waking?","cq":"寐如何？醒時其性如何？","good":1,"a":"Badly. And I wake up already annoyed, before anything has happened.","ca":"睡不好。醒來就已經在氣頭上，什麼事都還沒發生。","clue":{"en":"Poor sleep, irritable on waking","zh":"寐差，醒即煩躁"},"note":"Fire disturbs the spirit and the spirit does not settle. Note that this is <i>full</i> fire — restless and irritable with force behind it — as against the fretful, exhausted, hot-palmed insomnia of empty heat. 火擾神明，神不得安。當知此為<b>實</b>火：煩躁有力。與虛熱之失眠不同：彼則心煩而疲，手足心熱。"},{"q":"Do you have night sweats, or heat in your palms in the afternoon?","cq":"有盜汗否？午後手足心熱否？","good":1,"a":"No. I sweat when I shout, which is often, but not in my sleep.","ca":"沒有。喊戲的時候出汗，那倒常有，睡著不出。","clue":{"en":"No night sweats, no afternoon tidal heat","zh":"無盜汗，無午後潮熱"},"note":"An excellent negative, and the reason to ask it: night sweats plus tidal heat plus a red peeled tongue would have made this Lung and Kidney yin deficiency, where the blood in the cough comes from empty heat drying and damaging the vessels. Two patterns, both with hemoptysis, separated by whether the heat is full or empty. 問得其要。若見盜汗、潮熱、舌紅無苔，則當屬肺腎陰虛，其咳血為虛熱灼絡所致。二證俱見咳血，所別在熱之虛實。"},{"q":"Have you been eating cold food?","cq":"近日食冷否？","good":0,"a":"I eat whatever the troupe eats. Nothing has changed there.","ca":"班子吃什麼我吃什麼。飲食沒改過。","clue":{"en":"No change in diet","zh":"飲食如常"},"note":"Not useless, but not where this case lives. Diet is the question for the middle burner; this patient&#39;s trouble runs from the Liver upward to the Lung, and nothing in his story points at food. 非全無用，然非本案所在。飲食之問，為中焦而設。本案自肝上及於肺，其言無一涉於食。"}],"look":[{"t":"Red body, redder at the edges, with a yellow coating","c":"舌紅，邊尤紅，苔黃","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Red tongue with redder edges, yellow coat","zh":"舌紅邊尤紅、苔黃"},"note":"Correct, and the edges are the finding. The tongue is mapped: tip for the Heart, centre for Spleen and Stomach, root for the Kidney, and the two <b>edges</b> for Liver and Gallbladder. Edges redder than the body says the heat has an owner, and names it. Yellow coat confirms heat; a full yellow coat with a red body is excess heat rather than empty. 是，而所憑在邊。舌有分部：尖候心，中候脾胃，根候腎，兩<b>邊</b>候肝膽。邊紅甚於舌質者，言其熱有所屬，且指其名。苔黃主熱；舌紅苔黃而厚者實熱，非虛熱。"},{"t":"Red body with no coating at all","c":"舌紅無苔","ok":0,"note":"That is empty heat — yin so short that the coat cannot be grown. He has a solid yellow coat, which means there is something substantial and hot present, not merely something missing. This is the tongue that would have sent you to Lung and Kidney yin deficiency. 此虛熱之舌，陰虧而苔不能生。本案苔黃而實，是有形之熱在，非徒不足。見此舌者，當思肺腎陰虛。"},{"t":"Pale with a thin white coat","c":"舌淡苔薄白","ok":0,"note":"Cold or deficiency, and neither is on offer. Read it against the sleeve: a man bleeding from heat does not have a pale tongue. 主寒主虛，本案皆非。以袖上之血證之：熱而動血者，其舌不淡。"},{"t":"Purple with stasis spots","c":"舌紫有瘀點","ok":0,"note":"Blood stasis, which is a reasonable instinct when you see bleeding — but stasis bleeding is dark, clotted, fixed and stabbing, and does not answer to anger. His blood is bright and follows his temper. 主瘀。見血而思瘀，其思不謬；然瘀血之出色暗有塊，其痛固定如刺，不隨怒作。本案血鮮而隨其怒。"}],"pulse":[{"t":"Wiry and rapid 弦數","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Wiry rapid pulse","zh":"脈弦數"},"note":"Correct, and it is two organs in two syllables. <b>Wiry</b> — taut like a guitar string — is the Liver pulse, the pulse of constraint and of Liver excess. <b>Rapid</b> is heat. Wiry-and-rapid together is Liver fire, and here it is the pulse telling you which organ is driving a Lung symptom. 是，二字而及二臟。<b>弦</b>如按琴弦，肝之脈也，主鬱主肝實。<b>數</b>者熱。弦而數，肝火是也。本案肺見其症，而脈告其所由。"},{"t":"Floating and rapid 浮數","ok":0,"note":"Wind-heat on the surface — a new exterior illness with fever, sore throat and aversion to wind. His has run since spring, and there is nothing on the surface. 表有風熱，新病也，當見發熱、咽痛、惡風。本案自春徂今，且無表證。"},{"t":"Thready and rapid 細數","ok":0,"note":"Empty heat. <i>Thready</i> means the vessel is under-filled, which is the opposite of what a wiry pulse tells you. Deciding between wiry-rapid and thready-rapid is deciding between full fire and empty fire, and it changes every part of the treatment. 虛熱之脈。<b>細</b>者脈道不充，與弦相反。弦數與細數之辨，即實火虛火之辨，而治法全異。"},{"t":"Slippery 滑","ok":0,"note":"Phlegm, food, or pregnancy. There is phlegm here, but it is a by-product of the fire; the pulse is reporting the fire and the constraint, which is the more useful news. 主痰、主食、主孕。本案雖有痰，乃火之所生。脈所報者火與鬱，其用尤大。"}],"dx":[{"ask":"Step 1 — is this heat full or empty?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第一步：其熱為實，抑或為虛？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Full — the fire has force behind it","c":"實火 — 其熱有勢","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and there are four independent reasons. A <b>thick yellow coat</b> means something substantial is present, not merely something missing. A <b>wiry</b> pulse has tension and force. His irritability is loud rather than fretful. And there is <b>no</b> night sweating, no tidal heat, no afternoon flush. Empty heat is what remains when yin runs out; full fire is something generated and pushing. 是，其據有四。<b>苔黃而厚</b>，有形之邪在，非徒不足。脈<b>弦</b>則有力有勢。其煩為躁而非怯。且<b>無</b>盜汗、無潮熱、無顴紅。虛熱者陰竭而餘熱自見；實火者有所生而上迫。"},{"t":"Empty — yin is short and heat has surfaced","c":"虛火 — 陰虧而熱浮","ok":0,"note":"Then you would expect a red peeled tongue, a thready pulse, night sweats and heat in the palms after noon. He has a thick yellow coat, a wiry pulse, and no night sweats at all. Getting this wrong sends you to enrich yin in a man who needs the fire drained, and enriching herbs are cloying — they would make the phlegm worse. 若然，當見舌紅無苔、脈細、盜汗、午後手足心熱。本案苔黃而厚、脈弦、絕無盜汗。誤此則以滋陰治當瀉之火，而滋膩之品反助其痰。"},{"t":"Both at once","c":"虛實夾雜","ok":0,"note":"A safe-sounding answer that is not supported. Mixed patterns are real, but you diagnose them from evidence of both, and there is no evidence of yin depletion anywhere in this case. 此答似穩而無據。虛實夾雜誠有之，然必二者俱有其徵。本案通篇無陰虧之候。"},{"t":"Neither — this is blood stasis","c":"非熱，乃血瘀","ok":0,"note":"Stasis bleeding is dark and clotted, its pain is fixed and stabbing rather than burning, and it does not rise and fall with his temper. And it would not give a yellow coat. 瘀血之出色暗有塊，痛如針刺而固定，非灼，且不隨其怒而進退。亦不當見黃苔。"}]},{"ask":"Step 2 — follow the blood back. Why is there blood in a cough at all?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第二步：溯其血。咳而見血者，何故？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Fire has scorched the fine vessels of the Lung and driven blood out of them","c":"火灼肺絡，迫血妄行","ok":1,"note":"Correct. Heat does two things to blood: it makes it move faster and it damages the vessels holding it. The Lung is the most delicate of the organs and sits highest, so rising fire reaches it first and its fine vessels give way. That is why the bleeding follows his rages within the hour — the fire surges, the vessels are already scorched, and blood appears. 是。熱之於血有二：迫其行速，且灼其所藏之絡。肺為嬌臟而位最高，火炎上則先及之，其絡遂損。故每怒未逾一時而見血：火驟升，絡本已灼，血即隨之而出。"},{"t":"The Spleen has failed to contain the blood","c":"脾不統血","ok":0,"note":"A real mechanism and an important one — but it produces slow chronic bleeding in a pale, tired, poorly-eating patient with a weak pulse, and it bleeds downward and into the skin far more than upward. He is red-faced, forceful and furious. 此機誠有，且為要義。然其血緩而久，其人面白、神疲、納差、脈弱，且多下溢及肌膚，罕見上出。本案面赤、有力、善怒。"},{"t":"Empty heat from yin deficiency has dried and damaged the vessels","c":"陰虛虛熱，燥而損絡","ok":0,"note":"This is the other pattern that presents with hemoptysis, and separating the two is the examinable skill. Empty heat brings night sweats, tidal fever, a dry throat, a hoarse voice, a red peeled tongue and a thready rapid pulse. Full Liver fire brings anger, a bitter mouth, red eyes, a wiry rapid pulse and a thick yellow coat. He is entirely the second list. 咳血之另一證，二者之辨即考點所在。虛熱者：盜汗、潮熱、咽乾、聲嘶、舌紅無苔、脈細數。肝火實者：善怒、口苦、目赤、脈弦數、苔黃厚。本案全屬後者。"},{"t":"An exterior pathogen has injured the Lung","c":"外邪犯肺傷絡","ok":0,"note":"Exterior patterns are new, come with aversion to wind or cold, fever and a floating pulse, and they do not choose their moment according to who the patient has just argued with. 表證新起，兼惡風寒、發熱、脈浮，且不擇其人適與誰爭而發。"}]},{"ask":"Step 3 — you can cool the Lung or cool the Liver. Which, and why?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第三步：清肺乎？清肝乎？何以決之？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Cool the Liver first — it is the source; quiet the Lung as well","c":"先清其肝，以其為源；兼寧其肺","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and this is the difference between stopping an episode and ending a pattern. The Lung is where the symptom appears; the Liver is where the fire is made. Treat the Lung alone and you will stop this week&#39;s blood and see it again after the next argument. Treat the Liver alone and the scorched Lung vessels heal slowly and he keeps coughing. Do both, with the Liver leading — <i>clear the Liver, quiet the Lung</i>. 是。此止一發與絕一證之別。肺者見症之地，肝者生火之所。但治其肺，則本旬之血雖止，再爭必復。但治其肝，則已灼之肺絡愈遲，其咳不已。當二者兼治而以肝為主——清肝寧肺是也。"},{"t":"Cool the Lung only — that is where the blood is","c":"惟清其肺，血所出故","ok":0,"note":"Treating where the symptom shows rather than where it comes from. It will work, briefly, and it will keep working briefly for as long as he runs a theatre company. 治其所見而不治其所由。其效有之，然暫耳；彼一日司其班子，則一日如是。"},{"t":"Cool the Liver only — the Lung is innocent","c":"惟清其肝，肺無過也","ok":0,"note":"Half right, and the better half. But the Lung is not innocent — its vessels are already damaged and its qi is failing to descend, so it needs its own attention while the Liver cools. 得其半，且為要之半。然肺非無事：其絡已損，其氣不降，故當於清肝之時兼顧之。"},{"t":"Tonify the Lung so it can restrain the Liver again","c":"補肺以復其制肝之權","ok":0,"note":"Elegant five-phase reasoning and the wrong move in an excess pattern. Metal does normally restrain Wood, but you restore that relationship by taking the excess out of Wood, not by adding to a Lung that is currently being burned. Tonifying into fire is adding fuel. 五行之思甚精，而於實證非宜。金誠制木，然復其制者，當減木之有餘，非增被灼之肺。火中而補，猶益其薪。"}]}],"rx":[{"id":"LU6","t":"K&#466;ngzu&#236; LU6 孔最","ok":1,"note":"Cleft point of the Lung. Cleft points treat acute and painful conditions, and on the yin channels they also treat bleeding — so this is the point when a Lung pattern is bleeding <i>now</i>. It holds the blood while the Liver points remove the reason for it. 肺之郄穴。郄穴主急症痛症，陰經郄穴兼主血證，故肺病見血之際取之。此穴以止其血，而肝穴以去其所以出血者。"},{"id":"LR3","t":"T&#224;ich&#333;ng LR3 太衝","ok":1,"note":"Source point of the Liver, and here it is drained rather than tonified — it subdues rising Liver yang and fire and calms the temper that keeps restarting the case. In chapter thirteen the same point nourished a deficient Liver; the point is the same and the technique is opposite. 肝之原穴，於此當瀉而非補：平其上亢之陽與火，並息其屢起此證之怒。第十三章同穴而養其虛，穴同而法反。"},{"id":"LU5","t":"Ch&#464;z&#233; LU5 尺澤","ok":1,"note":"Sea point of the Lung and its Water point — the point that clears Lung heat downward. Sea points on the yin channels drain the organ&#39;s heat, and this is the standard choice for a hot, coughing Lung. 肺之合穴，屬水，清肺熱而下行之。陰經合穴主瀉其臟之熱，肺熱咳嗽之常法也。"},{"id":"ST36","t":"Z&#250;s&#257;nl&#464; ST36 足三里","ok":0,"note":"The great tonifying point, and tonifying is the objection. This is a full pattern with fire in it; strengthening now feeds what you are trying to remove. Save it for after the fire is out, if he turns out to be depleted underneath. 補之要穴，而所議在「補」。本證屬實而有火，補之適資其邪。俟火去而察其果虛，然後用之未晚。"},{"id":"KI7","t":"F&#249;li&#363; KI7 復溜","ok":0,"note":"Yesterday&#39;s point and yesterday&#39;s pattern — it warms Kidney yang. Warming a man whose tongue is red and whose ribs are burning is the clearest possible error of direction. 昨日之穴，昨日之證：溫腎陽者也。舌紅脅灼而溫之，寒熱之向，莫此為悖。"},{"id":"SP6","t":"S&#257;ny&#299;nji&#257;o SP6 三陰交","ok":0,"note":"A fine point for the three yin organs of the lower body, and not what this case needs — the trouble here runs upward from the Liver to the Lung, and none of it is deficient. 足三陰之要穴，然非本案所需。本案自肝上犯於肺，且無一虛處。"}],"tech":[{"t":"Reducing method, no moxa","c":"瀉法，禁灸","ok":1,"note":"<b>Correct, and there are two separate reasons to refuse the moxa.</b> First, heat: moxa is warm, and this is full fire. Second, bleeding: warmth makes blood move faster, and blood is already leaving the vessels. Either reason alone would be enough; together they make it a clear contraindication. Reduce, because there is something full to take away. 是，而拒灸之由有二。一者熱：灸性溫，本證實火。二者血：溫則血行愈疾，而血正妄行。二者有其一即當止，況兼之乎。當瀉者，以其有實可去也。"},{"t":"Reducing method, with moxa to move the stagnation","c":"瀉法，加灸以行其滯","ok":0,"note":"The reducing is right and the moxa undoes it. Moxa is withheld for heat, for dryness and for active bleeding — this case has two of the three, and the bleeding is the one you must never argue with. 瀉是而灸非，灸則廢其瀉。忌灸者：熱、燥、出血。本案居其二，而出血一項尤不可爭。"},{"t":"Reinforcing method, since he is exhausted from the season","c":"補法，以其一季勞倦","ok":0,"note":"He is tired, and tiredness is not the same as deficiency. Read the pulse: wiry and rapid, with force. A depleted patient does not produce that pulse. 其人誠倦，然倦不即虛。察其脈：弦數而有力。虛者不作此脈。"},{"t":"Even method, to be safe","c":"平補平瀉，取其穩","ok":0,"note":"Even technique is for mixed or unclear patterns. This one is neither — full fire, one direction, plain evidence. Choosing the middle here is not caution, it is undertreating a man who is coughing blood. 平補平瀉為虛實夾雜、證未明者設。本案不然：實火、一向、其據甚明。於此取中，非慎也，乃咳血而治不及。"}]};
